Sample Means" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fdff7ccc-5a47-5748-b28e-bcf8de0682a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163068 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163068 #edb/ref 1061 38034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163068 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fb9bf51-5dff-5207-8c2c-54d18ba6afd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163068 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163069 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163069 #edb/ref 1061 38036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163069 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0828c240-f63f-5c50-a2e8-eba11979ee4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163069 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1061 38037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ae3ab16a-d830-544c-8331-c62d1505d7dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1061 38040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c760552-8a9d-54b8-962b-87e6f849c3da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1061 38149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1061 38150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1065 6276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1064 "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1065 6300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1067 "The Standard Error" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1065 6301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8"